  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186087868,
      "id": "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44",
      "title": "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ccec811c-2ccf-5974-972b-bd45b2b96a66",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $3{y}^{′}-2{x}^{2}y+3x=3y+x$\n- $4{y}^{′}+3{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}=-2y+3{x}^{2}$\n- $4{y}^{′}+4{x}^{3}y-4x=-3y-4x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n3{y}^{′}-2{x}^{2}y+3x=3y+x.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side and the terms involving only $x$ on the right side, then divide through by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{′}-2{x}^{2}y+3x & =3y+x \\\\ 3{y}^{′}-2{x}^{2}y-3y+3x & =x \\\\ 3{y}^{′}+(-2{x}^{2}-3)y & =-2x \\\\ {y}^{′}+\\frac{-2{x}^{2}-3}{3}y & =\\frac{-2x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe forcing function is $Q(x)=\\frac{-2x}{3}$, which is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n4{y}^{′}+3{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}=-2y+3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side and the terms involving only $x$ on the right side, then divide through by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{′}+3{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2} & =-2y+3{x}^{2} \\\\ 4{y}^{′}+3{x}^{2}y+2y+3{x}^{2} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ 4{y}^{′}+(3{x}^{2}+2)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2}{4}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe forcing function is $Q(x)=0$, which is identically zero. Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n4{y}^{′}+4{x}^{3}y-4x=-3y-4x.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side and the terms involving only $x$ on the right side, then divide through by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{′}+4{x}^{3}y-4x & =-3y-4x \\\\ 4{y}^{′}+4{x}^{3}y+3y-4x & =-4x \\\\ 4{y}^{′}+(4{x}^{3}+3)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}+3}{4}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe forcing function is $Q(x)=0$, which is identically zero. Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
